    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's helpful, thanks. I was also wondering about the enzyme that kicks the whole process off. Can you explain the role of alliinase in this reaction? Basically, how does the damage to the cell structure actually trigger the enzymatic breakdown of the amino acid sulfoxides?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely. Alliinase is an enzyme found within the cell structure of the onion and other members of the Alliaceae family. When cells are damaged due to cutting, alliinase is released into the cell. It catalyzes the breakdown of the amino acid sulfoxides into sulfenic acids. These sulfenic acids quickly rearrange into syn-propanethial-S-oxide, the volatile compound responsible for onion irritation. Therefore, the enzymatic action of alliinase is what transforms innocuous sulfoxides into the irritant compound. Adequate lighting, careful handling, and perhaps even gentle cutting methods could help minimize cell damage and enzyme release.",
      "source": "target",
